<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Septimania</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Septimania was the coastal region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a>, roughly modern Languedoc-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a>. It was the only part of Gaul the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> kept after Vouillé in 507, was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> around 720, and was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a>, who captured Narbonne in 759; it then became a Carolingian march.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/septimania.jpg" alt="Septimania" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Polylerus at English Wikipedia. /; derivative work by Niczar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Septimania_in_537_AD.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The emirs sent regular summer campaigns against the north: under Hisham I armies sacked Oviedo in 794 and 795 and raided <b>Septimania</b> in 793, though the Franks took Barcelona in 801.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Their fusion with the Hispano-Romans produced a nobility that thought of itself as one people, the gens Gothorum or Hispani; its remnants fled to Asturias and <b>Septimania</b>, and in Asturias, Joseph F. O'Callaghan writes, joined the native leaders in a new aristocracy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Viscounts appear at Narbonne around 900 as deputies of the counts of Toulouse, who had inherited authority over the former Carolingian march of <b>Septimania</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/viscounty-of-narbonne">Viscounty of Narbonne</a></li></ul></section>
